 "readme": "# Finding Connections: Membership Inference Attacks for the Multi-Table Synthetic Data Setting\n\nThis repository contains the official implementation of the paper **\"Finding Connections: Membership Inference Attacks for the Multi-Table Synthetic Data Setting.\"** Our work explores how relational links between tables in synthetic datasets can be exploited to leak information about the membership of specific individuals in the private source data.\n\n## Getting Started\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n* Anaconda or Miniconda\n* Python 3.9+\n\n### Installation\n\n1. **Clone the repository:**\n\n2. **Create and activate the environment:**\n```bash\ncd MT-MIA\nconda env create -f environment.yml\nconda activate MT-MIA\n```\n\n---\n\n## Reproducing Paper Experiments\n\nTo replicate the results presented in the paper, we provide a suite of automation scripts.\n\n> **Note:** Generating multi-table synthetic data is computationally intensive. We have included pre-processed real data with fidelity metrics in `data/` and the pre-generated synthetic data used in our experimentation in `synth_data/` for ClavaDDPM, RealTabFormer, and RelDiff respectively. We also include the results of all MT-MIA and single-table attack runs generated by `./scripts/run_all_mtmia.sh` and `./scripts/run_all_single_table.sh` in the `results/` directory. To skip synthetic data generation, start from the third script below.\n\n> **RelDiff note:** Generating RelDiff synthetic data is GPU-compute intensive — our runs required approximately 450 GPU-hours on H200s. That said, RelDiff itself has low VRAM requirements and can be run on consumer-grade GPUs given sufficient time. Pre-generated RelDiff data is included in `synth_data/reldiff_synth/`.\n\nTo run the entire pipeline, download the original raw data for each dataset from the anonymous link https://figshare.com/s/7aa6f39fbab903ef7699 and place each `data/DATASET_NAME/original/` into their respective directories under `data/DATASET_NAME/`.\n\nThis Run the following scripts in order to replicate the paper:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Prepare the datasets\n./scripts/process_data.sh\n\n# 2. Generate synthetic data (ClavaDDPM + RealTabFormer)\n./scripts/generate_data.sh\n\n# 2b. Generate RelDiff synthetic data (GPU-intensive; ~450 GPU-hours on H200s)\n./scripts/generate_reldiff_airbnb.sh\n./scripts/generate_reldiff_airlines.sh\n./scripts/generate_reldiff_california.sh\n\n# 3. Execute Membership Inference Attacks\n./scripts/run_all_mtmia.sh          \n./scripts/run_all_single_table.sh    \n\n# 4. Evaluate synthetic data quality\n./scripts/run_fidelity_eval.sh\n\n```\n\nThe final tables and plots found in the paper can be generated using the Jupyter Notebooks located in the `notebooks/` directory.\n\n---\n\n## Using MT-MIA on New Datasets\n\nTo apply MT-MIA to your own synthetic relational data:\n\n1. **Format your data:** Follow the directory structure and schema format shown in `data/toy/`. For best results, ensure there are no missing data and categorical variables are ordinally encoded.\n2. **Configure your run:** Create a `.yaml` configuration file (refer to `configs/toy.yaml`).\n3. **Run the attack:**\n\n```bash\npython run_MTMIA.py --config configs/toy.yaml\n```\n",
